# Settings of the cyfr-locus image, read by the compose service `locus`.
# Copy to .env.locus to override defaults: one file for every role. Every
# setting of a Locus role is a LOCUS_RUNTIME_*, LOCUS_BUILDS_*,
# LOCUS_BACKENDS_* or LOCUS_LOG_* name, and the keeper hands each role
# process its own role's names and the log settings alone. Do not put
# application credentials, the keyring, a database URL, the runtime's root
# or the control plane's CYFR_LOCUS_BUILDS_KEY or CYFR_LOCUS_BACKENDS_KEY
# here: a Locus role refuses to start with any of them in its environment.
#
# The keeper starts the runtime's role process when it holds the runtime's
# key, the builds role when it holds a builds key and the backends role
# when it holds a backends key, each as a user of its own, in a space of
# its own. A role process that holds the runtime's key and either other
# key refuses to start: the runtime's runners have no network, so it
# shares no process, key or space with a role that has open egress. The
# keys are not set here. Compose passes the project .env's
# LOCUS_RUNTIME_KEY, the key `cyfr init` derives from the runtime's root
# for LOCUS_RUNTIME_SERVICE_ID, the project .env's CYFR_LOCUS_BUILDS_KEY,
# the key cyfr signs every build request with, as LOCUS_BUILDS_KEY, and the
# project .env's CYFR_LOCUS_BACKENDS_KEY as LOCUS_BACKENDS_KEY: each 32
# bytes as 64 hexadecimal digits, which `cyfr init` mints. A Locus run
# without compose is given its keys itself (integration-guide.md, "Running
# a worker outside Compose").
#
# The pool's bounds are not set here either: compose reads them from the
# project .env and the keeper writes them into its cgroups: the memory all
# of Locus's work shares, from LOCUS_CORE_RESERVE_BYTES and
# LOCUS_SERVICE_BYTES, and each role's processors, LOCUS_RUNTIME_CPU_LIMIT
# and LOCUS_BUILDS_CPU_LIMIT (.env.example has their defaults).

# ————— the release's log setting —————
#
# The level (a Logger level; default info) and format (`text`, or `json`
# for structured logs: Datadog, Splunk, ELK, Loki) of every role's process.
# A runner of the runtime reads neither: it logs at the default level, as
# plain text to standard error.
# LOCUS_LOG_LEVEL=info
# LOCUS_LOG_FORMAT=json

# ————— the runtime role —————
#
# What identifies the runtime is not set here: compose gives the role
# its id, its key and the URL of cyfr's host API from the project .env
# (documented in .env.example), and fixes the address and port its
# listener binds, over anything this file sets.

# The runner pool. Every subtree runs in a runner: a VM of its own under
# a pooled uid, started by cyfr-locus, holding no key. The service keeps
# this many fresh runners spawned ahead (each one a VM booting from
# nothing, so a bigger pool absorbs bursts at the cost of memory; the
# image has eight runner uids).
# LOCUS_RUNTIME_POOL_SIZE=4

# How long a runner that completed cleanly is kept idle for its athanor's
# next subtree, in milliseconds, before it is retired.
# LOCUS_RUNTIME_IDLE_TTL_MS=30000

# How far past its assignment's deadline a runner may live before it halts
# its VM, in milliseconds: the bound on a guest that ignores its deadline.
# LOCUS_RUNTIME_WATCHDOG_GRACE_MS=5000

# How long a runner being ended (a kill, an unclean completion) is given to
# report what it holds before its process group is killed, in milliseconds.
# LOCUS_RUNTIME_RELEASE_GRACE_MS=2000

# The memory bound cyfr-locus holds each runner to, in bytes: its VM,
# every guest's linear memory, the pages of its home and the kernel memory
# charged to it, together. A runner that reaches it is ended whole and
# never reused, and the service reports its open runs. A whole number of
# bytes from 16777216 (16 MiB) to 1099511627776 (1 TiB), the keeper's own
# range; there is no value that means no bound. The default is roughly
# twice the largest runner peak under the seed components, as
# tests/locus-runtime-image/memory.py --measure records it: room for two
# or three guests at the default 64 MiB of linear memory, not one at the
# 256 MiB ceiling, so a deployment that consents to more raises it.
#
# The bound needs the container's `security_opt: writable-cgroups=true`
# (docker-compose.yml; Docker Engine 28 or later on a cgroup v2 host).
# Without it cyfr-locus refuses to start, naming the option: no component
# runs, and none runs unbounded.
# LOCUS_RUNTIME_MEMORY_BYTES=402653184

# The CPU each runner may use, in microseconds of CPU time in every 100000
# (1000..102400000; the default is 100000, one CPU; 0 is refused), written
# to its cgroup beside its memory bound before it starts.
# LOCUS_RUNTIME_CPU_QUOTA=100000

# The runners start only through the cyfr-locus channel the keeper gives
# the role's process: a role started without it refuses to boot, naming
# cyfr-locus.

# Where the keeper's relays attach: a directory only the role's user can
# enter, on a tmpfs of its own. Unset, the role's run folder the keeper
# names (KEEPER_RUN_DIR, /run/locus/runtime).
# LOCUS_RUNTIME_ATTACH_DIR=/run/locus/runtime

# ————— the builds role —————

# The address and port the builds role listens on in its space. Its space
# has one route and its port is entered from the locus network alone, so
# every interface is that one; a Locus outside compose sets the one address
# to bind. cyfr reaches it at the host of CYFR_LOCUS_BACKENDS_URL on 4100.
# LOCUS_BUILDS_BIND=0.0.0.0
# LOCUS_BUILDS_PORT=4100

# Concurrent toolchain builds (cargo/npm: a CPU core and hundreds of MB
# each, for minutes), in all and for one athanor (1..1024). A build past
# either cap is refused as at capacity, never queued. Each build runs under
# a uid of its own from the image's pool of 16.
# LOCUS_BUILDS_MAX_CONCURRENT=2
# LOCUS_BUILDS_MAX_CONCURRENT_PER_TENANT=1

# A build's deadline in milliseconds (1000..600000). Past it every process
# the build started is killed and the build is refused as timed out.
# LOCUS_BUILDS_TIMEOUT_MS=270000

# A build's memory bound in bytes (16777216..1099511627776, 16 MiB to
# 1 TiB; the default is 1 GiB). Every build runs in a cgroup of its own
# held to this bound: its processes, what it writes to its home and the
# kernel memory charged to it, together, with no swap. A build that reaches
# it loses every process at once and is refused with the `memory` class,
# naming the bound; other builds and the role are untouched. There is no
# value that means "no bound". The shipped seeds build within 356-564 MiB
# (748 MiB with a cold cache).
#
# The bound needs the container's `security_opt: writable-cgroups=true`
# (docker-compose.yml; Docker Engine 28 or later on a cgroup v2 host).
# Without it cyfr-locus refuses to start, naming the option, and every
# build cyfr sends is refused as `unavailable`; none runs unbounded.
# LOCUS_BUILDS_MEMORY_BYTES=1073741824

# The CPU each build may use, in microseconds of CPU time in every 100000
# (1000..102400000; the default is 100000, one CPU; 0 is refused), written
# to its cgroup beside its memory bound before it starts.
# LOCUS_BUILDS_CPU_QUOTA=100000

# The role's processors are compose's, which reads them from the project
# .env, not from this file: LOCUS_BUILDS_CPU_LIMIT (default 2), split
# between its service and its builds. Its builds share the pool's memory
# with every other piece of Locus's work (LOCUS_CORE_RESERVE_BYTES in the
# project .env).

# The Cargo home whose registry cache a Rust build starts from. The image
# sets it to the seed it ships (/opt/cyfr/cargo-seed).
# LOCUS_BUILDS_CARGO_SEED=/opt/cyfr/cargo-seed

# ————— the backends role —————
#
# A Locus given LOCUS_BACKENDS_KEY serves the programs CYFR registers:
# 64 hexadecimal digits, the same key as the control plane's copy,
# CYFR_LOCUS_BACKENDS_KEY in the project .env. Each session of a program
# runs under a uid of its own from the keeper's `backends` pool, or, for a
# program whose home is kept, under its home's own uid.

# The address and port the backends role listens on in its space.
# CYFR_LOCUS_BACKENDS_URL, which compose sets for cyfr, names the same
# port.
# LOCUS_BACKENDS_BIND=0.0.0.0
# LOCUS_BACKENDS_PORT=4101

# The memory bound of a program whose row names none, in bytes
# (16777216..1099511627776, 16 MiB to 1 TiB; the default is 512 MiB),
# charged through the keeper's cgroup as a build's is: its processes, what
# they write to a scratch home and the kernel memory charged to them,
# together, with no swap. A session that reaches it loses every process at
# once; the next open starts it again after its backoff. There is no value
# that means "no bound". A row's own `memory_bytes` takes its place.
# LOCUS_BACKENDS_MEMORY_BYTES=536870912

# What the role admits: a session reserves its memory bound when it is
# opened, before its home is made, and holds it until its process is gone.
# The role opens a session only while the bounds it holds, with the new
# one, stay within LOCUS_BACKENDS_MEMORY_BUDGET times
# LOCUS_BACKENDS_MEMORY_OVERCOMMIT, rounded down, and while one athanor's
# stay within its ceiling (the athanor_program_memory_bytes platform
# setting, unset the budget itself); past either an open is refused as the
# box being full, and the person's program is asked to try again later.
#
# The budget is the memory the sessions may hold, in bytes
# (16777216..1099511627776; the default is 1879048192, 1792 MiB, three
# sessions at the default bound; 0 is
# refused). The sessions also share the pool's memory with every other
# piece of Locus's work (LOCUS_CORE_RESERVE_BYTES in the project .env).
# LOCUS_BACKENDS_MEMORY_BUDGET=1879048192
#
# The overcommit factor: how far the bounds may add up past the budget,
# a decimal of at least 1 (default 1, no overcommit). Most sessions use far
# less than their bound, so 1.5 admits half as many again; a box whose
# sessions then all grow at once has the kernel end one of them at the
# pool's bound. A factor below 1, or one that is not a plain decimal,
# refuses the boot.
# LOCUS_BACKENDS_MEMORY_OVERCOMMIT=1

# The CPU each session may use, in microseconds of CPU time in every
# 100000 (1000..102400000; the default is 200000, two CPUs; 0 is refused).
# The keeper writes it to the session's cgroup before its command starts,
# and refuses to start a session where it cannot.
# LOCUS_BACKENDS_CPU_QUOTA=200000

# Kept homes are not set here. Their folder and their uid range are the
# keeper's `--kept-homes <path>:<first>-<last>` in the image's entrypoint
# (Dockerfile.locus; by default /var/lib/locus/kept:40001-49999, on the
# `homes` volume), beside its `--pool`: the keeper alone finds, lists and
# draws kept homes, and a role names a home by its id.
